nv30: stop using the hardware index-buffer path on NV40

An indexed draw with a real index buffer binds it through the buffer context:
PUSH_RESRC/PUSH_MTHD register the method in the bufctx and write bo->offset +
data inline, using whatever offset is known at emit time -- 0 for a buffer this
submission has not validated yet. libdrm's pushbuf_validate() is what turns that
into a real address: it walks bctx->pending and appends the (method header,
value) relocation pair the kernel patches at submit time.

But the only validation an indexed draw gets runs inside nv30_state_validate(),
which nv30_draw_vbo() calls before nv30_draw_elements(). The index buffer is
bound after that, so its pair is never emitted and IDXBUF_OFFSET reaches the
engine as a bare, unrelocated resource offset -- 0x00000100 on the machine this
was found on, which is not even page aligned. The engine fetches indices out of
unrelated video memory and the garbage indices drive vertex fetches far past the
end of the vertex array bound at VTXBUF0/VTXBUF1: DMA_VTX_PROTECTION, on every
indexed VBO draw, deterministically.

The vertex and fragment-program bindings are registered during state validation
and so are covered by that validate, which is why they alone come back correctly
relocated in the very same push -- a controlled comparison inside one submission.

Validating again after the binding does emit the relocation and does eliminate
the faults -- measured, 16 relocations becoming 20 and zero faults over fifteen
consecutive runs of a reproducer that previously faulted every time. It is not
enough on its own. nv30_draw_vbo() reserves no pushbuf space between state
validation and the draw, so those extra dwords overrun the ring and the engine
reports DMA_PUSHER INVALID_CMD; and reserving space per batch instead puts a
flush point inside VERTEX_BEGIN_END, where nouveau_pushbuf_space() finishes a
flush by re-running validate, re-emitting vertex state inside an open primitive
and hanging the engine with no fault code at all.

NOTE: the space reasoning above is retracted. BEGIN_NV04/BEGIN_NI04 call
PUSH_SPACE(size + 1) internally -- NV50_PUSH_EXPLICIT_SPACE_CHECKING is only set
in nv50 query code, never for nv30 -- so this path was never missing a space
budget, and the real cause of the DMA_PUSHER INVALID_CMD those revisions produced
was never established. What is measured and reproducible is that emitting the
relocation from here destabilised the ring in several different ways, and that
the branch below is clean.

Rather than keep adding to a path that resists it, take the branch that has no
index buffer to relocate. The inline path is what nv30/nv34 and user
indices already use. It costs CPU -- indices go through the FIFO instead of by
DMA -- which on the affected hardware is a good trade for a driver that
otherwise faults on every indexed draw.

